module sh.sysl.solder

// The read-run-print loop, which is the same on every machine SOLDER runs on.
//
// **It lives in the language rather than in each console, because there is more than one console and
// there will be more.** Written out per platform, this loop would be several copies of twenty lines
// that drift: the one somebody uses daily grows a fix, the others do not, and the difference then
// reads as a *board* bug rather than as a console nobody updated.
//
// **`*Iterate[string]` is what makes one loop serve all of them.** A `sysl.term.edit.Editor` and a
// `sysl.io.Lines` are both one, so the platform decides which to build and nothing below that line
// knows the difference. What is left for a console is to name its streams and print its own banner.
//
// **The interpreter is a parameter rather than made here**, which is what makes this testable: a test
// hands it a cursor over a byte script and reads what the session left behind.

// Whether text ends where the next thing printed would start a line of its own.
//
// **The prompt is written from this rather than unconditionally after a newline**, because a Forth
// prints exactly what it was told to: `7 SQUARE PR CR` ends its own line and `7 SQUARE PR` does not,
// and a loop that always broke first would put a blank line under everything that used `CR`.
private ends_a_line(s: string) -> bool
    val b = s.bytes

    b.len == 0 || b[b.len - 1] == 10
end ends_a_line

// Every line the cursor yields, run, with whatever it printed shown as it goes.
//
// Returns when the cursor ends or when `BYE` is typed on a line of its own. **What to do about that
// is the caller's**, and the two answers differ: a hosted program returns from `main`, while a board
// has nowhere to exit *to* and says so rather than falling off the end.
//
// ## The continuation prompt, and why it is not the one a parsed language has
//
// **SOLDER's unfinished state lives in the interpreter rather than in this loop.** `DEF SQUARE` on
// one line and `DUP * END` on the next is a definition typed in two pieces; `5 0 DO I PR` and then
// `LOOP` is an anonymous one. Both work because `vm.compiling` and the body being built survive a
// call to `run` -- so the console asks `pending` and prints `....`, and there is nothing here to
// buffer.
//
// **A `More` is therefore a refusal here, not a continuation**, which is the opposite of what a
// language that parses a whole line before running any of it can do. A Forth executes as it reads: by
// the time an unterminated string literal is reached, the words before it have already run, and
// re-running the line with the rest appended would run them a second time. Reporting it and clearing
// what is half-built is the honest answer, and the case it costs -- a string literal split across two
// lines -- is one nobody types.
session(vm: &Vm, cursor: *Iterate[string])
    prints("solder> ")

    for line in cursor
        if !pending(vm) && line == "BYE" then break

        var fault = ""

        run(vm, line) match
            Ok(_) -> ()
            // Whatever the line printed before it went wrong is still owed to the reader: a `PR` that
            // ran is a `PR` that happened, and swallowing it would make what is on the screen
            // disagree with what the program did. So the output goes out first and the refusal after.
            Err(f) ->
                fault = describe(f)

                reset(vm)

        val out = drain(vm)

        prints(out)

        if !ends_a_line(out) then print("")
        if fault != "" then print(fault)

        prints(if pending(vm) then ".... " else "solder> ")

    print("")
end session
